   "page_count": 2,
   "pages": [
    {
     "page": 1,
     "text": "JOHNSON-JONES CLINIC - DISCHARGE SUMMARY\n\nPatient: Kathy Douglas DOB: 10/11/1976 Sex: F MRN: 564845\nAdmit: 10/31/2015\n\n---\n\n**Attending Physician:** Dr. Phyllis Jones, Hematology\n\n---\n\n**Admitting Diagnosis:** Anemia / blood disorders (venous thromboembolism, factor V Leiden mutation)\n\n**Principal Diagnosis:** Anemia / blood disorders (venous thromboembolism, factor V Leiden mutation)\n\n**Secondary Diagnoses:** None\n\n**Hospital Course:**\n\nMs. Kathy Douglas, a 49-year-old female, was admitted on October 31, 2015, with a diagnosis of anemia secondary to blood disorders, specifically factor V Leiden mutation with a history of venous thromboembolism (VTE). The patient has been under the care of Dr. Phyllis Jones at the Johnson-Jones Clinic since the onset of her condition in October 2015. \n\nUpon admission, Ms. Douglas reported experiencing symptoms consistent with a mild upper respiratory infection (URI), which included nasal congestion, sore throat, and a slight cough. She denied any recent episodes of VTE or other acute complications related to her anemia. \n\n**Vital Signs on Admission:**\n- Blood Pressure: 132/79 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 78 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute\n- Temperature: 98.6°F\n- Weight: 137 lbs\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- General: Alert and oriented, in no acute distress\n- HEENT: Normocephalic, atraumatic; clear nasal passages; throat erythematous\n- Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops\n- Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally, no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi\n- Abdomen: Soft, non-tender, no hepatosplenomegaly\n- Extremities: No edema, cyanosis, or clubbing\n\n**Laboratory Results:**\n- Complete Blood Count (CBC): Hemoglobin 11.2 g/dL, Hematocrit 33.6%, Platelets 220,000/µL, White Blood Cell Count 7,500/µL\n- Coagulation Profile: INR 1.1, aPTT 32 seconds\n- Basic Metabolic Panel: Within normal limits\n- D-Dimer: Slightly elevated at 0.5 µg/mL (normal <0.5 µg/mL)\n\n**Hospital Course:**\nMs. Douglas’s hospital course largely uneventful. She was managed conservatively with supportive care for her URI symptoms, which included rest, hydration, and over-the-counter analgesics as needed. Her anemia was monitored closely, and no acute interventions were required as her hemoglobin levels remained stable.\n\n**Procedures:**\n- performed during this admission\n\n**Discharge Medications:**\n- None specific for anemia; patient continues routine follow-up and management as an outpatient\n\n**Social History:**\n- Alcohol consumption: Approximately 10 drinks per week\n- Tobacco use: None\n- Occupational his"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "tory: Office worker\n\n**Follow-Up:**\nMs. Douglas was discharged home in stable condition on November 2, 2015. She was advised to follow up with her primary hematologist, Dr. Phyllis Jones, at the Johnson-Jones Clinic in two weeks for re-evaluation of her anemia and overall health status. She was also instructed to return to the emergency department if she experienced any signs of VTE, such as sudden shortness of breath, chest pain, or swelling in the extremities.\n\n**Patient Education:**\n- Importance of maintaining hydration and a balanced diet\n- Avoidance of prolonged immobilization to reduce the risk of VTE\n- Regular follow-up appointments to monitor her condition\n\n**Subsequent Visits:**\n- January 22, 2025: Visit for ankle sprain. Blood Pressure: 144/81 mmHg, Weight: 128 lbs\n- June 17, 2024: Routine follow-up for anemia. Blood Pressure: 144/89 mmHg, Weight: 141 lbs\n- November 10, 2023: Routine follow-up for anemia. Blood Pressure: 141/78 mmHg, Weight: 135 lbs\n- April 5, 2023: Routine follow-up for anemia. Blood Pressure: 143/84 mmHg, Weight: 130 lbs\n\nThroughout these visits, Ms. Douglas’s condition remained stable with no acute exacerbations of her anemia or VTE. She continued to adhere to her follow-up schedule and reported no significant changes in her overall health.\n\n---\n\n**Discharge Summary:**\nMs. Kathy Douglas was discharged in stable condition after a brief hospital stay for monitoring and management of her URI symptoms. Her anemia secondary to factor V Leiden mutation remained controlled, and no acute interventions were necessary. She was advised to continue her routine follow-up with Dr. Phyllis Jones at the Johnson-Jones Clinic and to maintain a healthy lifestyle to manage her condition effectively.\n\n---\n\n**Signature:**\nDr. Phyllis Jones, MD\nHematology\nJohnson-Jones Clinic\n\n---\n\n**Pages:** 6\n"
    }
   ],
